// A cooled turbine blade in a box of hot gas: the section a conjugate
// heat-transfer model is built on. The blade (mat 1, a nickel superalloy) is
// a thick, highly cambered airfoil -- 87 degrees of turning, 24% thick, with a
// rounded trailing edge -- pierced by four cylindrical cooling passages along
// its camber line (voids: the coolant is not meshed). A thermal-barrier
// coating (mat 2), 0.07 thick, covers it all the way round, trailing edge
// included, and the hot gas (mat 3) fills the box.
//
// What this domain is for. A thin layer closed on itself round a shape with
// both a tight convex turn and a long concave face. The coating is a ring
// (Euler characteristic zero) 3.6 elements thick at NP = 100 (h = 0.0196
// here), and at the trailing edge it wraps a blade that is itself only 3
// elements thick, so a layout has to carry the coating's structure through
// half a turn in a few elements, while the pressure side is concave over most
// of the chord. The gas is a second ring (the box minus the coated blade),
// and the blade is a region with four holes inside it. There are no
// junctions, and no corners but the box's. Nothing is a full-circle material region: the
// passages are voids.
//
// The airfoil is the NACA four-digit thickness distribution (24%, plus a
// linear term that leaves the trailing edge 4% thick) laid along a
// quadratic-Bezier camber line, closed by a semicircle and staggered -35
// degrees; the coating's outer face is its exact outward offset. Both are
// written as Splines through samples 0.012 apart. Each passage keeps at least
// 3.9 elements of alloy between itself and the coating.

// ---- generated geometry ----
